WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.201
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:02.201 --> 00:00:03.669
We are here with
Kumail Nanjiani.

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:06.038
So, last time we spoke, you were
promoting "The Eternals."

00:00:06.038 --> 00:00:07.240
You got in superhero shape to

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.908
enter the
Marvel cinematic universe.

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:11.277
This was a very different
weight change

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:13.546
for this show to prepare for
Steve Banerjee.

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:16.415
-I'm the only one on the show

00:00:16.415 --> 00:00:19.985
who stays completely clothed
the entire time.

00:00:19.985 --> 00:00:23.490
And it was a joy.
It was so good.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.693
Even my one sex scene,
I have my shirt on.

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:28.795
-Whoo!

00:00:28.795 --> 00:00:31.164
-Very mixed --
-Very mixed.

00:00:31.164 --> 00:00:32.431
Like, what are they saying?

00:00:32.431 --> 00:00:33.566
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:34.767
-Are they whooing for
the sex scene

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:35.835
or the fact that
I kept my shirt on?

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:37.536
-Yeah, yeah.

00:00:37.536 --> 00:00:39.339
"Whoo! We don't have to see
all of it!"

00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:43.175
-Yeah. "Whoo! It's implied!"

00:00:43.175 --> 00:00:46.045
"Whoo! I'll imagine it!"

00:00:46.045 --> 00:00:47.313
-But you did, though --

00:00:47.313 --> 00:00:48.681
You know,
we could even see from the clip,

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:52.018
you obviously had, you know,
your cast of actors

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:53.853
who played male dancers
in the show.

00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:54.854
Obviously,
they're stripping down,

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:56.823
and you had to do
many dance scenes

00:00:56.823 --> 00:00:57.823
over the course of filming it.

00:00:57.823 --> 00:00:59.826
-Yes.
-How were those to do?

00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:01.293
-You know what
was great about those?

00:01:01.293 --> 00:01:03.395
So, we had,
like, fabulous actors

00:01:03.395 --> 00:01:05.297
who are also, like,
Broadway-trained dancers

00:01:05.297 --> 00:01:07.233
who were
the dancers on the show.

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.602
And, you know,
when you sort of do --

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:12.205
When you're recording it -- So,
we had, like,

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:14.006
background artists,
like, you know, 100 in the room.

00:01:14.006 --> 00:01:15.442
And they're all women,

00:01:15.442 --> 00:01:19.145
because men were not allowed in
to see the show back then.

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:20.813
And because
when you're recording,

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:22.214
you don't want
the sound to disrupt

00:01:22.214 --> 00:01:23.650
whatever dialogue is happening.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:26.052
So we would tell the audience
to, like, mime the reaction,

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:28.454
so they'd just be,
like, doing that.

00:01:28.454 --> 00:01:31.190
But every time
they yelled "cut,"

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:32.926
they would, like, explode.

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:36.028
Every single time.
They were like, "Yeah!"

00:01:36.028 --> 00:01:39.732
Day after day, week after week,
the same audience.

00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:42.201
Anytime somebody, like,
ripped off their pants,

00:01:42.201 --> 00:01:43.402
you'd hear, like, a...
[ Gasps ]

00:01:43.402 --> 00:01:46.072
...like a gasp every time.

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:48.741
Like you know
what's under there.

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:51.377
You saw it five minutes ago.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.279
There was one -- You know,
there was one part where

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:54.580
one woman,
one background artist,

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.784
has to, like, rip off one of
the stripper guy's pants,

00:01:57.784 --> 00:02:01.221
and she turned into
an animal every time.

00:02:01.221 --> 00:02:03.423
Every time, she was like, "Aah!"

00:02:03.423 --> 00:02:04.690
And we'd yell cut,
and she'd come to like,

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:05.725
"Okay, what'd I miss?

00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:07.393
What happened?
When do we shoot?"

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:09.328
We're like,
"We've done 30 takes.

00:02:09.328 --> 00:02:12.198
You've been great every time."

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:13.800
She turned into an animal.
It was great.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.301
And you see like,
"Oh, this is why

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:17.737
Chippendales was
such a success,"

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:22.609
because it created the space
where women could, in public,

00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:24.076
show a part of themselves
that they weren't

00:02:24.076 --> 00:02:26.779
allowed to until then,
so it was really, really --

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:28.781
-It was ahead of his time
in a real genuine way.

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:29.815
-It really was.

00:02:29.815 --> 00:02:31.217
It was really exciting,
you know?

00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:34.220
It was like,
energy was up every time.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.821
-You --
We were talking backstage.

00:02:35.821 --> 00:02:37.990
You were a producer
on this, as well.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:41.794
You know, so many more
responsibilities as --

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:42.929
You know,
you've been a writer before.

00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:43.963
Obviously,
you've been an actor before.

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:46.298
Did you enjoy the producer side?

00:02:46.298 --> 00:02:49.435
-It is so good to be
involved with all parts of it,

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:51.337
but then you kind of
don't have an excuse.

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:52.638
You know what I mean?
-Yeah.

00:02:52.638 --> 00:02:54.340
-Because I've acted in movies,
and I watch it,

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.610
and I'm like,
"Oh, no, why did they..."

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:58.778
You know what I'm talking about.

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:02.915
Go look at my IMDb,
all the green splats.

00:03:02.915 --> 00:03:06.886
All the green splats --
that's their fault.

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:09.221
This one, if it's a green splat,
it's my fault.

00:03:09.221 --> 00:03:10.256
-Yeah, that's your fault.

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:11.324
-Yeah,
'cause you're always like,

00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:12.525
"Why did they use that take?

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:13.893
Why did they do this."
Da-da-da.

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:16.562
But here -- But I love
the editing part of it the most

00:03:16.562 --> 00:03:18.630
because you don't have to get up
early in the morning,

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.566
and you can just,
like, fix everything, you know?

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:21.634
It's great.

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:23.536
-I mean, I think,
certainly in comedy,

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:24.937
when you so often --

00:03:24.937 --> 00:03:27.139
By the time you've said it,
it's already too late.

00:03:27.139 --> 00:03:29.842
It must be nice to actually get
in and be granular with it.

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:33.479
-Yes. And sometimes with comedy,
you're shooting a scene.

00:03:33.479 --> 00:03:34.881
You know, you're doing
a scene of comedy,

00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:36.582
and you shoot it like
20, 30 times.

00:03:36.582 --> 00:03:37.983
By the end,
it's not funny at all,

00:03:37.983 --> 00:03:40.085
and you're like, "I don't think
this is funny anymore."

00:03:40.085 --> 00:03:42.555
And then, when you watch it,
you can really be like,

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:44.390
"Oh, no,
it was funny the whole time.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:46.024
We had just gotten,
like, sick of it."

00:03:46.024 --> 00:03:49.295
Like if I made another,
you know, Ents joke...

00:03:49.295 --> 00:03:50.396
-It wouldn't go as well.

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:51.630
-...it wouldn't go as well as --

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:54.600
You know, the first time,
it crushed.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.668
Also, I realized
we did that in break,

00:03:56.668 --> 00:03:58.470
so that -- This is --

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.940
-I'm really heartbroken
for our at-home audience.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:04.176
During the break, Kumail did
a really funny joke about

00:04:04.176 --> 00:04:07.246
the tree people from
"Lord of the Rings."

00:04:07.246 --> 00:04:09.849
And when -- I mean,
there's no way to prove it.

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:11.784
[ Cheers and applause ]
They remember.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:15.287
-That was not the reaction
the first time.

00:04:15.287 --> 00:04:17.289
-No. This is maybe --

00:04:17.289 --> 00:04:20.192
I mean, so much went --
So, you made --

00:04:20.192 --> 00:04:21.527
Let's just track what happened.

00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:24.263
You made an Ents joke
that nobody laughed at.

00:04:24.263 --> 00:04:28.100
Then you called it back,
realizing that the people

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.136
who were watching at home didn't
see it bomb in the first place.

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:31.304
-That's right.

00:04:31.304 --> 00:04:33.772
-So you somehow did
a worse version of a joke

00:04:33.772 --> 00:04:35.441
that got no laughs
the first time.

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:38.611
-Yes. I did a callback
to a bomb joke.

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:40.746
And you know what?
Kind of killed with it.

00:04:40.746 --> 00:04:43.682
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:43.682 --> 00:04:44.984
What is that?

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:47.086
What is that about?

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:48.221
-Yeah.
-How did that happen?

00:04:48.221 --> 00:04:49.856
-I mean, that is --
I mean, the way

00:04:49.856 --> 00:04:52.024
you're doing comedy these days
is so interesting.

00:04:52.024 --> 00:04:56.195
Yeah. I just do callbacks.
I don't do the first one.

00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:57.864
Who has time for
the first punch line?

00:04:57.864 --> 00:04:59.264
-Yeah. Everybody likes
the callbacks

00:04:59.264 --> 00:05:00.566
more than the first --

00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:01.867
-That's true.
-Yeah.

00:05:01.867 --> 00:05:03.502
-Yeah.
I go right to the third tag.

00:05:03.502 --> 00:05:04.937
-This is very much like,

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:08.340
"Why don't they make the whole
plane out of the black box?

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:09.976
-Except for,
like, way less relatable.

00:05:09.976 --> 00:05:11.244
-Yeah.

00:05:11.244 --> 00:05:13.612
-I think this might be
just for me and maybe you?

00:05:13.612 --> 00:05:14.814
I'm not sure.

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:16.048
-This part that we're doing
right now?

00:05:16.048 --> 00:05:18.084
-Yeah, I think it might --
-I think mostly you.

00:05:18.084 --> 00:05:20.286
[ Laughter ]

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:22.087
I take it back.
I'm enjoying it, too.

00:05:22.087 --> 00:05:24.957
-You explained a callback
to a joke

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:27.292
that we had to explain
before we did the joke.

00:05:27.292 --> 00:05:30.462
-Yeah. Every part of this,
I feel like, is special,

00:05:30.462 --> 00:05:34.733
and I don't feel like you can
get it anywhere else, you know?

00:05:34.733 --> 00:05:35.968
-Yeah. I don't know why.

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:37.136
-You're not -- Will you please

00:05:37.136 --> 00:05:38.804
do a special called
"Just Callbacks"?

00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:40.506
-It's just the callbacks.
-Yeah.

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:43.075
-That's actually a great idea.

00:05:43.075 --> 00:05:44.443
I'm just, like,
referencing things

00:05:44.443 --> 00:05:46.078
that I never referenced
in the first place.

00:05:46.078 --> 00:05:48.213
-Yeah, but,
like, really confidently,

00:05:48.213 --> 00:05:49.315
like it's going to land.

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:52.018
-Right. Or, like,
doing a catchphrase

00:05:52.018 --> 00:05:53.485
that I've never done before,

00:05:53.485 --> 00:05:55.855
but just, like, landing it like
it's a catchphrase,

00:05:55.855 --> 00:05:59.325
like, "Now, that's what
the thing is."

00:05:59.325 --> 00:06:00.660
Is that a good catchphrase?

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.061
-I bet you could
get away with it.

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:03.595
-"That's what the thing is"?
-"That's what the thing is."

00:06:03.595 --> 00:06:05.932
Maybe workshop that part.

00:06:05.932 --> 00:06:08.467
-That part is my catchphrase.
-What?

00:06:08.467 --> 00:06:09.535
-That part.

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:11.203
-You know what you
should call it?

00:06:11.203 --> 00:06:14.006
"That part 2."
-That's right.

00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:15.741
[ Cheers and applause ]
That's it.

00:06:15.741 --> 00:06:19.078
That's what it is.
Just the callbacks.

00:06:19.078 --> 00:06:21.447
-Thank you for being here.
Congratulations on the show.

00:06:21.447 --> 00:06:23.249
It's really cool.
And it's great to see you.

00:06:23.249 --> 00:06:24.884
-Oh, man, always --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:26.085
-Kumail Nanjiani, everybody.

00:06:26.085 --> 00:06:27.787
"Welcome to Chippendales"
is streaming now on Hulu.

00:06:27.787 --> 00:06:29.055
We'll be right back with
Matt Rogers.

